WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.950
A coroutine runs on some thread, but suspend doesn't say which.

2
00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:12.438
The dispatcher in its context picks it, each time the coroutine starts or resumes.

3
00:00:12.438 --> 00:00:15.383
This is Kotlin only: Java has no coroutines.

4
00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:20.797
When you name no dispatcher, launch and async use Dispatchers.Default.

5
00:00:20.797 --> 00:00:23.795
It has one thread per core, and at least two.

6
00:00:23.795 --> 00:00:26.927
On a 4-core pod that's 4 threads, for CPU work.

7
00:00:27.067 --> 00:00:34.272
Run a 200 millisecond JDBC query there, and the thread sleeps for 200 milliseconds, doing nothing.

8
00:00:34.272 --> 00:00:39.197
Four requests doing that take every thread, and all CPU work waits.

9
00:00:39.933 --> 00:00:53.209
In the lesson's lab, 32 such calls take about 1,600 milliseconds, 8 rounds of 4, and a tiny CPU task waits about 1,500 milliseconds to start.

10
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:58.641
Blocking calls belong on Dispatchers.IO, which runs up to 64 at once.

11
00:00:58.641 --> 00:01:04.187
Those threads mostly sleep, waiting for Oracle, so 64 on 4 cores is fine.

12
00:01:04.933 --> 00:01:12.343
There, the 32 calls take about 220 milliseconds, and the tiny task starts within a few milliseconds.

13
00:01:13.267 --> 00:01:16.291
IO and Default share one pool of worker threads.

14
00:01:16.291 --> 00:01:23.349
The split gives them separate budgets: blocking work can fill IO, and Default still has a thread for every core.

15
00:01:23.533 --> 00:01:26.824
To move one piece of work, use withContext.

16
00:01:26.824 --> 00:01:29.962
Loading the rows is a blocking JDBC call.

17
00:01:29.962 --> 00:01:32.257
Rendering the PDF is CPU work.

18
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:36.118
withContext suspends the caller and runs the block on IO.

19
00:01:36.118 --> 00:01:43.293
Often the same worker keeps going, now counted against IO's limit, and Default's place goes to another thread.

20
00:01:43.467 --> 00:01:49.575
The query sleeps for 200 milliseconds, and Default's four threads keep doing CPU work.

21
00:01:49.733 --> 00:01:55.381
When the block is done, the caller resumes on its own dispatcher, Default, with the rows.

22
00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:58.173
Rendering is CPU work, so it runs there too.

23
00:01:59.067 --> 00:02:06.941
So never block on Default: wrap each blocking call in withContext(Dispatchers.IO), inside the function that blocks.

24
00:02:06.941 --> 00:02:10.707
Calls that already suspend, like delay, need no switch.

25
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:21.403
When one resource needs its own budget, limitedParallelism makes a view of IO with its own limit, like 20 for a card issuer with 20 connections.

26
00:02:21.403 --> 00:02:26.475
A view owns no threads, and it adds to IO's 64 instead of taking a share.
